{"archetypes":[{"name":"quiet-fd","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff changes Python ship driver stdout/stderr routing, fd 3 contract output, journaling, and top-level error handling.","prompt_body":"Investigate the quiet-mode and contract-stream changes in the Python ship driver. Check whether result JSON still reaches the intended caller stream across normal completion, argparse failures, Python-version failures, internal exceptions, and BrokenPipe scenarios. Also verify that breadcrumbs and tracebacks are routed consistently without leaking contract output into quiet logs or losing actionable diagnostics. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"stall-state","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff changes finalize-state persistence, restoration, stall metadata fallback, and stall-recovery classification inputs.","prompt_body":"Review the finalize-state and stall-recovery state machine changes across Python and shell. Check preservation and precedence of STALL_TRACKING, STALL_STEP, EXIT_CODE, BAIL_REASON, and related PR metadata when ship state, finalize state, and session env disagree. Pay special attention to gap-fill behavior after stalled outcomes and whether recovery can misclassify or overwrite useful evidence. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"shell-quoting","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff introduces shell-quoted finalize-state values and hand-written shell unquoting/parsing paths.","prompt_body":"Examine the new shell quoting and unquoting behavior for finalize-state and related state files. Look for injection, malformed parsing, newline handling, single-quote edge cases, and divergence between Python shlex parsing and shell sed/awk readers. Consider whether untrusted state-file contents can affect finalization, restoration, or logging commands in unsafe ways. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"artifact-globs","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan centers on dynamic Codex artifact inclusion/exclusion and the diff modifies larch-log artifact glob policy and tests.","prompt_body":"Inspect the round artifact inclusion globs and their regression tests. Verify that dynamic Codex outputs and permitted sidecars are included while prompts, vote prompts, telemetry, placeholders, dirty-tree artifacts, and raw static specialist transcripts remain excluded. Check ordering-sensitive glob interactions, especially broad output patterns that may override narrower denials. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-runner","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff centralizes many test runners into a shared RecordingRunner, which can hide ordering or strictness regressions.","prompt_body":"Review the shared RecordingRunner migration and affected tests. Check whether previously distinct runner behavior, call recording, default responses, strict failures, keyed responses, and side-effect expectations are preserved. Look for tests that now pass too easily because unexpected subprocess calls receive successful defaults. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
